\subsubsection*{A note on Assignment 8}

Run your function definitions first; use the task-specific checks below as you implement them. The checks do not assess your simulation experiments.

The algorithm checks use fixed rewards, deterministic transitions, or properties that hold for any sampled action. The reward-generator checks use sample means and standard deviations with tolerances for sampling variation. They do not require the same random choices as the examples. Different correct implementations can consume random numbers or break ties differently, so their outputs can differ even with the same seed.

\newpage

\input{oral_exam_question_contribution.tex}

\newpage

\section{Bandits}

In the first part, we will study the $k$-armed bandit problems more in detail.

\examplebox{Dynamic web page example application}{
A website chooses which of five headlines to show. Each headline is an action; a click gives reward 1 and no click gives reward 0. The website observes the response only to the headline shown, so it must balance using promising headlines with trying others. Changing reader interests motivate the non-stationary setting.
}

\begin{enumerate}
\item (2p) \textbf{Implementation (1p).} Implement a stationary 5-armed bandit reward function that generates a reward $R$ for a given category according to a true underlying (stationary) reward. Implement a function \texttt{stationary\_bandit(a, t = NULL)} that takes an action $\mathcal{A} = (1,...,5)$ and ignores the optional time argument \texttt{t}. The reward function should return a reward according to
\[
R \sim N(q^\star_a, 1)\,,
\]
where $q^\star=(1.62, 1.20, 0.70, 0.72, 2.03)$. Below is an example of how it \emph{could} work. Note that since the function returns a random value, you might get a different result, and it might still be a correct implementation. However, to test, you can run your bandit 1000 times and take the mean (see below). Then you should be close to $q^\star$ if your implementation is correct.
\textbf{Example (1p).} Include inputs, a call, seed and compact actual output from your implementation, with a brief explanation. Reuse a supplied example or the required experiments.

<<stationary-example, eval=FALSE>>=
set.seed(4711)
stationary_bandit(2)
## [1] 3.019735
stationary_bandit(1)
## [1] 2.99044
R <- numeric(1000)
for (i in 1:1000) {
    R[i] <- stationary_bandit(3)
}
mean(R)
## [1] 0.6819202
@
\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "stationary_bandit")|
\newpage
\item (2p) \textbf{Implementation (1p).} Implement a non-stationary reward function. For all actions $\mathcal{A} = (2,...,5)$ the non-stationary should work as the stationary reward function. Although for action $a=1$, the function should return a reward based on the iteration according to
\[
R \sim N(-3 + 0.01t, 1)\,.
\]
Implement the reward function as \texttt{nonstationary\_bandit(a, t)} and it should work as follows. Again, note that these are random values, you might have another (correct) solution giving different results.
\textbf{Example (1p).} Include inputs, a call, seed and compact actual output from your implementation, with a brief explanation. Reuse a supplied example or the required experiments.

\begin{samepage}
<<nonstationary-example, eval=FALSE>>=
set.seed(4711)
nonstationary_bandit(2, t = 1)
## [1] 3.019735
nonstationary_bandit(2, t = 1000)
## [1] 2.57044
nonstationary_bandit(1, t = 1)
## [1] -1.793682
nonstationary_bandit(1, t = 1000)
## [1] 6.593121
@
\end{samepage}
\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "nonstationary_bandit")|
\newpage
\item (2p) \textbf{Implementation (1p).} Implement a greedy policy algorithm that always chooses the greedy action, according to Eq. (2.2) in \citet{sutton2020reinforcement}. Use sample-average value estimates. The function called \texttt{greedy\_algorithm(Q1, bandit)} should input a vector of initial estimates $Q_1$ and a reward function. Then the algorithm should run the bandit for 1000 steps and return a list containing (1) The rewards $R_t$ for all steps, (2) the mean reward over all the 1000 steps, (3) the value estimates $Q_t(a)$, and (4) the total number of choices of each action $N_t(a)$. Here is an example of how it could work. \emph{Note!} This is a random algorithm so you might get a different result, even if you use the exact same seed and have a correct algorithm.
All bandit algorithms below should request each reward with \texttt{bandit(a, t = i)}, where \texttt{i} is the current step (1 to 1000); both reward functions accept this call.
\textbf{Example (1p).} Include inputs, a call, seed and compact actual output from your implementation, with a brief explanation. Reuse a supplied example or the required experiments.

<<greedy-example, eval=FALSE,size="footnotesize">>=
Q1 <- rep(0, 5)
set.seed(4711)
greedy_algorithm(Q1, stationary_bandit)
## $Qt
## [1] 1.602744 0.000000 0.000000 0.000000 0.000000
##
## $Nt
## [1] 1000    0    0    0    0
##
## $R_bar
## [1] 1.602744
##
## $Rt
## [1] 3.439735 2.990440 2.816318 1.213121 ...

set.seed(4711)
Q1 <- rep(0, 5)
greedy_algorithm(Q1, nonstationary_bandit)
## $Qt
## [1] -1.170265  1.180905  0.000000  0.000000  0.000000
##
## $Nt
## [1]   1 999   0   0   0
##
## $R_bar
## [1] 1.178554
##
## $Rt
## [1] -1.1702649  2.5704395  2.3963182  0.7931208 ...
@

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "greedy_algorithm")|

\begin{hintblock}{Checking returned results}
Check the number of rewards, total action count, and reported mean:
<<result-check-hint, eval=FALSE>>=
result <- greedy_algorithm(rep(0, 5), stationary_bandit)

stopifnot(
  length(result$Rt) == 1000,
  sum(result$Nt) == 1000,
  isTRUE(all.equal(result$R_bar, mean(result$Rt)))
)
@
No output means these checks passed. Reuse them for the later bandit algorithms.
\end{hintblock}
\item (2p) \textbf{Implementation (1p).} Implement the $\epsilon$-greedy algorithm.

Use \texttt{epsilon\_algorithm(Q1, bandit, epsilon)}. With probability $\epsilon$, choose an action uniformly from all five actions; otherwise choose a greedy action. Keep the greedy algorithm's updates and output structure.
