\section{Markov Decision Processes}

In a Markov Decision Process (MDP), the agent chooses an action $A_t$ based on the current state $S_t$. The environment then produces the next state $S_{t+1}$ and reward $R_{t+1}$. We use the recycling robot MDP \citep[Example 3.7 in ][]{sutton2020reinforcement}.

We write $p_{\mathrm{high}}$ for the probability of remaining high after searching from high energy, and $p_{\mathrm{low}}$ for the probability of remaining low after searching from low energy. These correspond to the transition probabilities called $\alpha$ and $\beta$ in the textbook.

\begin{enumerate}
\item Use \texttt{recycling\_mdp(p\_high, p\_low, r\_wait, r\_search)} in the \texttt{uuml} R package to access the MDP in \citet{sutton2020reinforcement}. Use version 0.5.0 or later; reinstall using the instructions at the start of the assignment if needed.
<<mdp-example, eval=FALSE>>=
library(uuml)
mdp <- recycling_mdp(p_high = 0.5, p_low = 0.8, r_wait = 0.1,
    r_search = 1)
mdp
##       s s_next        a p_s_next r_s_a_s_next
## 1  high   high   search      0.5          1.0
## 2  high    low   search      0.5          1.0
## 3   low   high   search      0.2         -3.0
## 4   low    low   search      0.8          1.0
## 5  high   high     wait      1.0          0.1
## 6  high    low     wait      0.0          0.1
## 7   low   high     wait      0.0          0.1
## 8   low    low     wait      1.0          0.1
## 9   low   high recharge      1.0          0.0
## 10  low    low recharge      0.0          0.0
@
\examplebox{Recycling robot transitions}{
With $p_{\mathrm{high}}=0.5$ and $p_{\mathrm{low}}=0.8$ in the call above:

\textbf{Search with high energy:} 50\% probability of remaining high and 50\% of becoming low. Either transition gives reward 1.

\textbf{Search with low energy:} 80\% probability of remaining low with reward 1; 20\% of requiring rescue, returning to high energy with reward $-3$.

}
\newpage
\item (2p) \textbf{Implementation (1p).} Implement \texttt{always\_search\_policy(mdp)} to simulate 1000 actions, starting in state \texttt{high} and always choosing \texttt{search}. Return a list with mean reward \texttt{R\_bar} and state counts \texttt{Nt}, named \texttt{high} and \texttt{low}. Count the state before each action.
\textbf{Example (1p).} Include inputs, a call, seed and compact actual output from your implementation, with a brief explanation. Reuse a supplied example or the required experiments.

\begin{samepage}
<<search-policy-example, eval=FALSE>>=
set.seed(4711)
always_search_policy(mdp)
## $Nt
## high  low
##  288  712
##
## $R_bar
## [1] 0.452
@
\end{samepage}

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "always_search_policy")|

\item (2p) \textbf{Implementation (1p).} Implement \texttt{charge\_when\_low\_policy(mdp)} using the same initial state, run length, and output structure. Choose \texttt{recharge} when energy is low and \texttt{search} when it is high.

\textbf{Example (1p).} Include inputs, a call, seed and compact actual output from your implementation, with a brief explanation. Reuse a supplied example or the required experiments. Reuse a policy run from the comparison below.

